#Runbook: Check EC2 Details and Access from Terminal

## Purpose
This task is about checking important EC2 details directly from the terminal without opening the AWS Management Console. It also confirms whether the logged-in Linux user has admin access or only limited SSH access.[1][2][3]

## Step 1: Check who is logged in
Run these commands after SSH:

```bash
whoami
groups
```

`whoami` shows the current Linux user. `groups` shows which groups the user belongs to. If groups like `sudo` or `wheel` are missing, the account usually does not have admin rights.[2][3]

## Step 2: Check sudo access
Run:

```bash
sudo -l
```

If the output says the user is not allowed to run sudo, then the account has limited permissions. This means SSH access is available, but system-level changes like installing packages are blocked.[3][4]

## Step 3: Check EC2 metadata
AWS provides instance details from inside the EC2 through the metadata service at `169.254.169.254`.[1][5]

To see available metadata:

```bash
curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/
```

Useful commands:

```bash
curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/instance-id
curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/instance-type
curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/ami-id
curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/placement/availability-zone
```

These commands show the instance ID, instance type, AMI ID, and Availability Zone. The region can be understood from the Availability Zone, for example `ap-south-1a` means region `ap-south-1`.[1][6][7]

## Step 4: Check IAM role
To see whether an IAM role is attached:

```bash
curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/
```

If a role name is shown, that means the EC2 has an attached IAM role.[8][9]

## Final understanding
If the user can SSH into the EC2 but cannot run sudo, install software, or make system changes, then the account is a non-admin Linux user with limited permissions. This is usually intentional and follows least-privilege access.[2][3][10]
